window.__DIYDB__=window.__DIYDB__||{data:{}};window.__DIYDB__.data["bronco6g"]=[{"id":"bronco6g-rear-locker","title":"Rear Locker Activation \u2014 Badlands and Sasquatch","vehicle":"bronco6g","category":"axles","subcategory":"factory-systems","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.1,"high":0.25},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":[],"skills_required":["transfer-case mode awareness"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2021-2026"],"tags":["axles","factory","badlands","sasquatch","locker","off-road"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Bronco Nation \u2014 When to Use Bronco's Front or Rear Locker","url":"https://thebronconation.com/news/when-to-use-bronco%E2%80%99s-front-or-rear-locker-t.23024/"},{"title":"Ford.com \u2014 2026 Bronco Badlands Electronic Locking Axles","url":"https://www.ford.com/suvs/bronco/models/bronco-badlands/image/electronic-locking-front-and-rear-axle/"},{"title":"The Drive \u2014 Manual Bronco Base Sasquatch Review","url":"https://www.thedrive.com/car-reviews/ford-bronco-sasquatch-manual-review"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The electronic rear locker on Badlands and Sasquatch trucks is a single button on the dash hero switches. Press it once with the truck in 4H or 4L below roughly 20 mph and a straight steering wheel. The amber locker icon means engaged. The rear axle now drives both wheels at exactly the same speed regardless of traction. Use it whenever you need it; turn it off before pavement and tight turns.","description":"The 6G's electronic rear locker lives in the rear Dana M220 axle on Badlands trim and on every Sasquatch-equipped truck. It's not a limited-slip; it's a true mechanical locker that mechanically couples both rear wheels. With it engaged, a wheel lifted in the air can't spin uselessly \u2014 the wheel still on the ground gets full torque.\n\nThe hero switch for the rear locker is the second-from-right button on the row above the screen for a rear-only setup (Badlands without Sasquatch), or one of two adjacent locker buttons if you also have the Sasquatch-package front locker. The icon shows a rear axle with the X-marker between the wheels.\n\nEngagement conditions are simple but enforced. The transfer case must be in 4H or 4L \u2014 the truck refuses to engage the locker in 2H. Vehicle speed should be at or under 20 mph; some trucks accept the request a bit higher but the actuator may not engage until you slow further. Keep the steering close to centered when you press the button. If the icon flashes amber, the system has accepted the request but the actuator hasn't latched yet \u2014 keep driving slowly and straight and it'll latch within a couple of wheel rotations. Solid amber means locked.\n\nWhen to use it: anytime you cross-axle (one rear wheel in the air, one on the ground), anytime one rear wheel is on slick rock or ice and the other has grip, and any climb where the rear is breaking traction unevenly. The factory open differential without the locker will throw all available torque to the wheel with the least grip \u2014 exactly the wrong wheel. The locker fixes that.\n\nWhen NOT to use it: on dry pavement, on tight turns of any kind, or above the speed at which it'll disengage. A locked rear axle forces both wheels to rotate at the same speed, so in a turn the inside wheel must either skip or the outside wheel must scrub. On pavement that destroys tires and stresses driveline parts. Above its disengage speed (around 25\u201330 mph in most conditions) the system will automatically unlock \u2014 but don't rely on automatic disengage; turn it off intentionally before transitioning back to road driving.\n\nSasquatch trucks add the front locker on the M210. Use them in sequence: rear first, then add the front only if the rear alone doesn't move you. The front locker compromises your turning radius significantly because the front axle is now forcing both front wheels to rotate together \u2014 even in low-speed dirt that's a noticeable difference.\n\nIf the locker icon flashes and never goes solid, the actuator solenoid may be stuck or the differential clutch isn't seating. Cycling 4H to 4L and back, or rocking the truck a few feet forward and back, usually frees it. Persistent failure to engage warrants a dealer scan \u2014 the system stores DTCs.","steps":["Identify the rear locker button on the dash hero switches (rear axle icon with X between wheels).","Shift transfer case to 4H or 4L. Slow to under 20 mph.","Straighten the steering wheel.","Press the rear locker button once. Watch for amber icon.","If icon flashes, keep moving slowly and straight \u2014 wait for it to go solid.","Use the locker through the obstacle.","When clear, press the button again to disengage. Confirm icon goes dark before turning or accelerating.","If the icon refuses to go solid, try cycling 4H/4L or rocking the truck a couple of feet. Persistent failure = dealer scan."],"pros":["True mechanical locker, not a limited-slip. Cross-axle situations get you out instead of stranding you.","Button operation lets you lock and unlock mid-obstacle without leaving the seat.","Standard equipment on Badlands and any Sasquatch-package truck \u2014 no aftermarket spending needed."],"cons":["Pavement and tight turns destroy tires with the locker engaged. Always unlock before returning to road.","Forgetting to unlock before a turn at speed can damage axle hardware. The dash icon is your reminder; don't ignore it.","Non-Badlands, non-Sasquatch 6Gs have an open rear differential. Adding a locker aftermarket is a real install, not a flash-and-go upgrade."]},{"id":"bronco6g-front-pads-rotors","title":"Front Brake Pad and Rotor Replacement","vehicle":"bronco6g","category":"brakes","subcategory":"pads-rotors","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":2.5},"cost_usd":{"low":180,"high":380},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","18mm + 21mm sockets","torque wrench (0-150 ft-lbs)","C-clamp or caliper piston tool","wire brush","brake cleaner","lug wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","brake service"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2021-2026"],"tags":["brakes","safety-critical","service"],"parts":[{"name":"Motorcraft front brake pads (ceramic)","part_number":"BRF-1801","vendor":"Ford / Amazon","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Centric Premium front rotors (pair)","part_number":"120.65177","vendor":"RockAuto / Amazon","price_estimate_usd":130},{"name":"Brake caliper grease packet","vendor":"Permatex / CRC","price_estimate_usd":6}],"citations":[{"title":"Bronco6G forum \u2014 brake rotor specs and replacement thread","url":"https://www.bronco6g.com/forum/threads/brake-rotor-specs-replacing.87515/"},{"title":"Bronco6G forum \u2014 torque spec reference list","url":"https://www.bronco6g.com/forum/threads/torque-spec-reference-list.89889/"},{"title":"Level Up Suspension \u2014 2021+ Ford Bronco torque specs","url":"https://www.levelupsuspension.com/pages/2021-ford-bronco-torque-specs"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Front pads and rotors on a 6G Bronco run 1.5\u20132.5 hours in the driveway. Rotor nominal thickness is 34mm and the discard spec is 22mm \u2014 once you measure under 22mm, replace, don't resurface. Caliper bracket bolts torque to 129 Nm (about 95 ft-lbs).","description":"This is a safety-critical job. If you're new to brake work, read the whole procedure before starting and consider doing one side at a time so the assembled side stays as a reference. The 6G's front brakes are conventional dual-piston floating calipers \u2014 same general layout as F-150, easy to service, parts widely stocked.\n\nYou'll know it's time when the pad indicator squeals at low speed, the pedal feels longer than it used to, or you see less than 3mm of pad material left behind the wheel spokes. Rotor replacement is recommended any time you replace pads if the rotor is at or near minimum thickness, has heat-cracked surfaces, or has a measurable lip on the outside edge. Trail use (especially water crossings followed by hard descents) warps rotors faster than highway driving.\n\nPick a flat surface, chock the rear wheels, break the lug nuts loose with the wheels on the ground, then jack the front and set stands under the frame rails. Pull both front wheels. The caliper is held by two slider bolts on the back (15mm head, often with a flat to hold the slider pin); remove those and lift the caliper off \u2014 don't let it dangle by the hose, support it with a hook or zip-tie to the coil spring. Pads slide out of the bracket. To remove the rotor, the bracket bolts off (two large 21mm bolts at the back of the knuckle, 129 Nm spec). The rotor lifts free \u2014 sometimes with a tap from a dead-blow hammer if rust has welded it to the hub.\n\nWire-brush the hub mating surface clean \u2014 rust here is the most common cause of brake judder after a rotor swap. Set the new rotor on, hand-thread one lug nut to hold it. Bolt the bracket back on at 95 ft-lbs (129 Nm). Compress the caliper piston with a C-clamp or piston tool \u2014 open the bleeder first so old fluid pushes out into a rag rather than back into the master cylinder. Slide new pads into the bracket, lube the slider pins lightly with caliper grease, and reinstall the caliper. Torque the slider bolts to 23 ft-lbs (31 Nm).\n\nReinstall the wheels, lug nuts at 150 ft-lbs (204 Nm) in a star pattern. Before driving, pump the brake pedal slowly until it firms up \u2014 it will feel scary-soft on the first one or two strokes after a piston compression. Bed the pads in with 8\u201310 stops from 35 mph down to 5 mph, then a five-minute cool-down at speed without dragging the brakes.","steps":["Break lug nuts loose with the truck on the ground.","Lift the front, set jack stands under the frame.","Remove both front wheels.","Remove the two 15mm caliper slider bolts.","Lift the caliper off; hang it from the spring with a hook or zip-tie.","Slide the pads out of the bracket.","Remove the two 21mm bracket bolts; pull the bracket.","Pull the rotor (tap with a dead-blow if seized).","Wire-brush the hub face clean of rust.","Install the new rotor; hand-thread one lug nut.","Reinstall the bracket; torque to 95 ft-lbs (129 Nm).","Open the caliper bleeder; compress the piston with a C-clamp.","Install new pads with lubed slider pins.","Reinstall the caliper; torque slider bolts to 23 ft-lbs.","Close bleeder. Repeat on opposite side.","Reinstall wheels; torque lugs to 150 ft-lbs in star pattern.","Pump the brake pedal until firm before moving the truck.","Bed the pads: 8\u201310 stops from 35 mph to 5 mph, then cruise to cool."],"pros":["Conventional brake layout \u2014 no electronic parking brake retraction needed up front.","Strong aftermarket support: Centric, EBC, PowerStop, Brembo, Hawk all make 6G front kits.","95 ft-lb bracket bolt spec is reasonable \u2014 anyone with a $30 torque wrench can hit it."],"cons":["Brakes are safety-critical. If you've never bled a system or compressed a piston, watch a full video first and don't skip the pedal-pump step before driving.","6G rotors are larger and heavier than older Broncos \u2014 set them down carefully, not on edge.","Rotor judder after replacement almost always traces back to rust on the hub face that wasn't cleaned. Don't skip that step."]},{"id":"bronco6g-air-filter","title":"Engine and Cabin Air Filter Replacement","vehicle":"bronco6g","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"filters","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":0.5},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":65},"tools_required":["flathead screwdriver","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2021-2026"],"tags":["service","filters","ecoboost"],"parts":[{"name":"Motorcraft FA-2022 engine air filter (2.3L / 2.7L)","part_number":"FA-2022","vendor":"Ford / Amazon","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Motorcraft FP-93 / CAF6 cabin air filter (MB3Z-19N619-A)","part_number":"FP-93","vendor":"Ford / Amazon","price_estimate_usd":24}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ Pony Parts \u2014 Motorcraft FA-2022 air filter for 2.3L/2.7L Bronco","url":"https://www.cjponyparts.com/ford-motorcraft-air-filter-2-3l-2-7l-bronco/p/ACE39/"},{"title":"CJ Pony Parts \u2014 Motorcraft FP-93 Bronco cabin air filter","url":"https://www.cjponyparts.com/ford-motorcraft-cabin-air-filter-bronco/p/CAF6/"},{"title":"Buckle Up Off-Road \u2014 Bronco cabin air filter listing","url":"https://buckleupoffroad.com/products/cabin-air-filter"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The engine air filter is the Motorcraft FA-2022 (2.3L and 2.7L both); the cabin filter is the Motorcraft FP-93 / CAF6. Both are 5-minute swaps with no tools beyond a flathead screwdriver \u2014 replace the engine filter every 30,000 miles and the cabin filter every 15,000, or sooner if you drive dirt.","description":"Trail dust eats air filters. A clogged engine filter robs throttle response and forces the MAF sensor to work harder; a clogged cabin filter loads the HVAC blower and pumps unfiltered desert grit into your face. Neither one is expensive to replace and both are jobs anyone can do without lifting the truck.\n\nThe engine air filter sits in a black plastic airbox on the passenger side of the engine bay, just behind the headlight. Four metal spring clips hold the lid down. Pop them open (no tools \u2014 just fingers, sometimes a flathead to start a stubborn clip), lift the lid, and the filter comes straight up. The FA-2022 is a flat panel rectangle \u2014 note orientation before pulling it. Wipe the airbox base clean of dust and leaves before dropping the new filter in, then snap the lid back. If you live somewhere dusty (Arizona, New Mexico, Utah trail country), an aFe Pro Dry S, K&N E-2997, or AEM Brute Force panel filter is a drop-in reusable upgrade. Don't oil reusable filters too heavily \u2014 over-oiling fouls the MAF sensor and triggers a check engine light.\n\nThe cabin filter lives behind the glove box. Open the glove box, then squeeze both sides inward to release the dampers, and let it drop fully open. A horizontal plastic door is visible on the right side of the cabin \u2014 pull the small tab to release it. The old filter slides out (it folds slightly to clear). Note the airflow arrow on the new filter \u2014 usually pointing down toward the floor \u2014 and slide it in. Close the door, reseat the glove box.\n\nIf your truck has been daily-driven on dirt roads, pull the old cabin filter outside before disposal. Pollen, pine needles, and rodent droppings shake out aggressively. Wear gloves.","steps":["*Engine air filter (FA-2022):**","Open the hood. Locate the airbox on the passenger side behind the headlight.","Release the four metal spring clips around the airbox lid.","Lift the lid; remove the old filter.","Wipe the airbox base clean.","Drop in the new filter (note orientation arrow).","Reseat the lid and snap the clips closed.","*Cabin air filter (FP-93):**","Open the glove box fully.","Squeeze both glove box sides to release the dampers; let it drop.","Pull the tab on the horizontal filter access door.","Slide the old filter out (fold slightly to clear opening).","Install the new filter with the airflow arrow pointing down.","Reseat the access door and the glove box."],"pros":["No tools required (cabin filter); only finger-strength for the airbox clips.","Cheap parts, fast service \u2014 easy to bundle with an oil change.","Major impact on cabin air quality after dusty trail days."],"cons":["Aftermarket reusable engine filters are a real upgrade in dusty climates but require careful oiling to avoid MAF sensor faults.","Cabin filter location behind the glove box means the glove box has to be fully released \u2014 first-time owners find this awkward.","Cabin filter intervals are easy to forget. Set a reminder, or stack the swap with every other oil change."]},{"id":"bronco6g-coolant","title":"Coolant Service Intervals \u2014 EcoBoost","vehicle":"bronco6g","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"cooling","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":110},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","drain pan (\u22653 gallons)","10mm socket","8mm socket","funnel","shop rags","OBDII bleed procedure (optional)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","EcoBoost cooling system awareness"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2021-2026"],"tags":["service","fluids","cooling","ecoboost"],"parts":[{"name":"Motorcraft Yellow P-OAT Concentrate \u2014 1 gal","part_number":"VC-13DIL-G2","vendor":"Ford / Amazon","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Distilled water \u2014 1 gal (mix 50/50)","vendor":"Grocery","price_estimate_usd":3}],"citations":[{"title":"Ford Support \u2014 What engine coolant should I use","url":"https://www.ford.com/support/how-tos/more-vehicle-topics/engine-and-transmission/what-engine-coolant-should-i-use-in-my-vehicle/"},{"title":"Bronco Nation \u2014 2021 Ford Bronco recommended fluids","url":"https://thebronconation.com/the-recommended-fluids-filters-and-components-of-the-2021-ford-bronco/"},{"title":"Bronco6G forum \u2014 coolant level diagnosis thread","url":"https://www.bronco6g.com/forum/threads/engine-coolant-help-found-the-level-far-below-the-minimum-line.71200/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The 6G ships with Motorcraft Yellow P-OAT coolant (Ford spec WSS-M97B57-A1) rated for 10 years / 200,000 miles. Stay with Yellow. If anyone tops it off with orange, the service window collapses to 6 years / 100,000 miles. EcoBoost cooling systems trap air pockets \u2014 plan the refill carefully.","description":"Ford's published interval on the 6G is genuinely long because Yellow P-OAT is durable chemistry. The bigger risks are not interval \u2014 they're contamination, mixing, and air pockets after a refill.\n\nThe cooling system holds a few gallons depending on engine. The 2.3L EcoBoost runs roughly 2.5 gallons total. The 2.7L EcoBoost V6 runs roughly 3 gallons. Capacities are not aggressively published by Ford; the practical answer is \"have a full gallon of Yellow concentrate plus a gallon of distilled, and have a second gallon staged in case you under-fill.\"\n\nTwo failure modes drive most coolant trouble on the 6G. First, mixing chemistries \u2014 adding orange (G-05 HOAT) or universal \"green/gold/red works in any car\" coolant to the yellow factory fill cuts the chemical life in half. Use Motorcraft Yellow concentrate mixed 50/50 with distilled water, or buy the pre-mixed jug. Second, air pockets. EcoBoost engines (especially the 2.7L) trap air in the heater core loop. The dash will read normal temp while a pocket of air slowly cooks the cylinder head.\n\nThe bleed procedure: with engine cold, top off the degas bottle. Run the heater to MAX HEAT, fan on low. Start the engine and let it idle until the upper radiator hose is hot to the touch (thermostat open). Squeeze the upper hose periodically to burp trapped air back to the degas bottle. Top off as needed. Let it cool overnight and recheck the cold-fill line on the bottle. Expect to add half a quart over the first few heat-cool cycles.\n\nThe coolant level on the 6G is read on the degas bottle on the passenger side of the engine bay. The MIN and MAX lines are stamped into the plastic and easy to miss in poor light \u2014 use a flashlight at the bottle level. Low coolant on a 6G is one of the most common forum issues at delivery; if your truck is consistently below MIN, look first at the upper radiator hose clamp (a known under-torqued spring clamp from the factory) and at the degas bottle cap seal.","steps":["Wait until the engine is fully cold. Park level.","Remove the degas bottle cap to break vacuum on the system.","Lift the truck. Place a 3-gallon drain pan under the lower radiator hose connection.","Loosen the lower radiator hose clamp at the radiator and pull the hose. Let it drain fully (15\u201320 minutes).","Reinstall the lower hose, hand-tight clamp; torque clamp to spec.","Mix 50/50: half Yellow concentrate, half distilled water. Pre-mix in a clean jug.","Fill the degas bottle to MAX with the mix.","Set HVAC to MAX HEAT, fan on low. Start the engine. Idle.","As thermostat opens, squeeze the upper hose to purge air. Top off as level drops.","Run for 20\u201330 minutes, watching temp gauge. Shut down.","Let cool overnight. Top off to cold-fill line. Recheck after first drive cycle."],"pros":["Yellow P-OAT genuinely lasts 10 years if you don't contaminate it. Topping off with the wrong color is the only common reason to service early.","The degas bottle is easy to read. Catching low level early prevents an EcoBoost head from warping.","Distilled-water + concentrate mix is the cheapest, longest-life approach."],"cons":["Air pockets in the 2.7L heater core loop will eat a head if missed. The bleed procedure is not optional.","The degas bottle MIN/MAX marks are nearly flush with the bottle surface \u2014 easy to misread without good light.","Mixing yellow with orange (or any other chemistry) cuts service life to 6 years. There is no shortcut here."]},{"id":"bronco6g-differential-fluid","title":"Differential Fluid \u2014 Dana M210 / M220","vehicle":"bronco6g","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"drivetrain","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":140},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","3/8\" ratchet (square drive for fill plug)","fluid pump","drain pan","torque wrench","RTV silicone (if removing cover)","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2021-2026"],"tags":["service","fluids","drivetrain","axles"],"parts":[{"name":"Motorcraft 75W-85 gear oil (rear M220) \u2014 2 qts","part_number":"XY-75W85-QL","vendor":"Ford / Amazon","price_estimate_usd":32},{"name":"Motorcraft 80W-90 gear oil (front M210) \u2014 1 qt","part_number":"XY-80W90-QL","vendor":"Ford / Amazon","price_estimate_usd":16},{"name":"Differential cover gasket or RTV (if pulling cover)","vendor":"Permatex Ultra Black","price_estimate_usd":8}],"citations":[{"title":"Bronco6G forum \u2014 driveline fluid change discussion","url":"https://www.bronco6g.com/forum/threads/change-driveline-fluids-early.83929/"},{"title":"Bronco6G forum \u2014 75W85 rear differential gear oil thread","url":"https://www.bronco6g.com/forum/threads/75w85-rear-differential-gear-oil-what-are-you-using.81564/"},{"title":"Bronco Nation \u2014 differential fluid type discussion","url":"https://thebronconation.com/general-bronco-discussion-n.139/okay-so-got-my-diff-covr-from-jegs-but-which-fluid-help-t.8918/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The 6G runs a Dana M210 front (26.2 oz, Ford spec 80W-90) and a Dana M220 rear (65.3 oz, Ford spec 75W-85 \u2014 63.6 oz with the electronic locker). The factory fluid is pulled out within the first 5,000 miles by most informed owners because it ships dark and full of break-in metal.","description":"Ford spec calls for the first scheduled diff service at 150,000 miles. Trail-driven 6Gs see metallic clutch and gear shed in the first few thousand miles and the differential fluid is visibly contaminated long before any service interval. Change it once at 3,000\u20135,000 miles, again at 30,000, then every 30,000 thereafter or after any deep water crossing.\n\nFord specifies Motorcraft 80W-90 for the front M210 and Motorcraft 75W-85 for the rear M220. Dana itself recommends 75W-140 in both axles \u2014 that's heavier than Ford's spec and adds thermal margin if you're towing or running 35s on highway. For a stock or mildly modified truck driven in Phoenix or anywhere hot, stay with the Ford spec; for 37s, heavy load, or sustained dragstrip use, 75W-140 in the rear is defensible. Use synthetic in both either way.\n\nNeither axle has a drain plug. Fluid removal is either through a suction pump from the fill hole or by pulling the diff cover. The cover route is messier but lets you wipe magnet shavings, inspect ring-and-pinion wear, and start clean. Most 6G owners go cover-off the first time, then suction-pump subsequent services.\n\nPark level, lift the truck, set stands so the axle stays level. Loosen the fill plug FIRST \u2014 the rear plug is a 3/8\" square drive on the cover face. If the fill plug won't break loose, do not drain. Once the fill plug is open, either pump fluid out from above or remove the 10 cover bolts in a star pattern and let the cover drop. Wipe everything inside clean with a lint-free rag, clean the magnet, scrape old gasket / RTV off both sealing surfaces, lay a fresh bead of Permatex Ultra Black (3\u20134mm wide, looping inside the bolt holes), bolt the cover back at 25 ft-lbs in a star pattern, and wait 30 minutes for the RTV to skin before filling.\n\nRefill through the fill hole until fluid runs back out \u2014 that's the level. Front M210 takes about 26 oz of 80W-90; rear M220 takes 63\u201365 oz of 75W-85 (the exact number depends on whether you have the Badlands/Sasquatch electronic locker). Reinstall fill plugs at 25 ft-lbs.","steps":["Lift the truck so the axle sits level; set jack stands.","Loosen the fill plug FIRST (3/8\" square drive). Confirm it breaks free.","Place drain pan under the cover.","EITHER suction fluid from the fill hole, OR remove 10 cover bolts in a star pattern and let cover drop.","If cover off: wipe interior clean, clean magnet, scrape old sealant from both surfaces.","Lay a 3\u20134mm RTV bead on the cover, looping inside each bolt hole.","Reinstall cover; torque bolts to 25 ft-lbs in star pattern. Let RTV skin 30 minutes.","Pump fresh fluid into the fill hole until it runs back out.","Reinstall fill plug at 25 ft-lbs.","Repeat on the opposite axle.","Drive 5 miles and recheck for seep."],"pros":["Cleaning the magnet at the first service tells you exactly how the axle is breaking in \u2014 fine fuzz is normal; metal flakes are not.","Synthetic gear oil at correct intervals heads off ring-and-pinion wear, the #1 cause of premature axle failure in trail-driven trucks.","The job uses basic tools \u2014 no specialty equipment if you go suction-pump route."],"cons":["Ford service intervals (150,000 miles) are far too long for off-road use. The truck doesn't warn you when the fluid is destroyed.","Pulling the cover is messy. Plan for a soaked drain pan and shop floor without a layer of cardboard.","Confirming the fill plug breaks loose BEFORE draining is the most-skipped step. A stripped fill plug strands you with an empty axle."]},{"id":"bronco6g-oil-change-2-3","title":"Oil Change \u2014 2.3L EcoBoost","vehicle":"bronco6g","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"oil","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.75,"high":1.25},"cost_usd":{"low":55,"high":95},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","13mm socket","oil filter wrench","drain pan","torque wrench","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2021-2026"],"tags":["service","fluids","ecoboost","2-3l"],"parts":[{"name":"Motorcraft FL-910S oil filter (spin-on)","part_number":"FL-910S","vendor":"Ford / Amazon","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"6 qt Motorcraft 5W-30 full synthetic (WSS-M2C961-A1)","vendor":"Ford / Walmart","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Drain plug crush washer","vendor":"Motorcraft","price_estimate_usd":3}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ Pony Parts \u2014 Motorcraft FL-910S for 2.3L Bronco","url":"https://www.cjponyparts.com/ford-motorcraft-oil-filter-2-3l-ecoboost-bronco/p/FL910S/"},{"title":"Bronco6G forum \u2014 upgrading the 2.3 oil filter","url":"https://www.bronco6g.com/forum/threads/upgrading-the-2-3-oil-filter.55135/"},{"title":"Ford service content \u2014 2.3L EcoBoost capacities and specifications","url":"https://www.fordservicecontent.com/Ford_Content/vdirsnet/OwnerManual/Home/Content?variantid=3618&Uid=G1724921"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The 2.3L EcoBoost takes 6.2 quarts of 5W-30 full synthetic with a Motorcraft FL-910S spin-on filter \u2014 a 45-minute service that you should run every 5,000 miles on a daily-driven 6G, or every 3,000 if the truck sees real dust and water.","description":"Ford's published interval for the 2.3L EcoBoost is 10,000 miles under \"normal\" conditions and 5,000 under severe. If your Bronco crawls Sedona, fords Salt River, or sits parked in 110-degree Phoenix summers, you're severe service \u2014 period. Short oil intervals are cheap insurance against the carbon and fuel dilution that turbocharged direct-injection fours are known for. Run synthetic, run it on time, and you sidestep most of the headline EcoBoost failures.\n\nThe factory fluid is Motorcraft Full Synthetic 5W-30 meeting Ford spec WSS-M2C961-A1. Mobil 1 Annual Protection, Pennzoil Platinum, and Valvoline Restore & Protect in the same viscosity all meet or exceed the spec. The 2.3L is one of the simpler EcoBoosts to service \u2014 single inline turbo, conventional spin-on filter on the driver-side of the block, drain plug visible from below.\n\nPark on level ground and run the engine 5 minutes to warm the oil. Shut down, lift the front, and set jack stands under the frame. Crawl under, pull the engine splash shield (eight 8mm bolts and two push pins), and the drain plug sits at the rear of the pan \u2014 13mm hex, ~22 ft-lbs of torque with a fresh crush washer. Drain takes 3\u20135 minutes. Spin the FL-910S off by hand or with a strap wrench, wipe the sealing surface clean, smear a film of fresh oil on the new filter's gasket, and hand-tighten three-quarters of a turn past first contact. Refill with 6.2 quarts, run the engine 30 seconds, recheck the dipstick after settling, and inspect for leaks before dropping the truck.\n\nReset the oil life monitor through the SYNC instrument cluster menu: Settings \u2192 Vehicle \u2192 Oil Life Reset \u2192 Hold OK.","steps":["Warm the engine 5 minutes, then shut off.","Lift the front and set jack stands under the frame rails.","Remove the engine splash shield (8mm bolts + push pins).","Position a drain pan; remove the drain plug (13mm).","While draining, spin off the FL-910S filter.","Install the new filter with oiled gasket, hand-tighten 3/4 turn past contact.","Replace the drain plug with new crush washer, torque to 22 ft-lbs.","Reinstall the splash shield.","Lower the truck. Add 6.2 quarts of 5W-30 full synthetic.","Start engine for 30 seconds, shut off, wait one minute, check dipstick, top to full.","Reset the oil life monitor through SYNC."],"pros":["Straightforward service \u2014 no special tools beyond a 13mm and a strap wrench.","The FL-910S filter is cheap (~$12) and stocked at every parts counter.","Quality synthetic at 5,000-mile intervals heads off most known EcoBoost problems."],"cons":["The splash shield adds 5 minutes vs older trucks with open underbodies.","Direct-injection EcoBoosts can dilute oil with fuel \u2014 stretching intervals to 10K is asking for problems on a hard-used truck.","Mishandling the crush washer or over-torquing the drain plug into the aluminum pan is an expensive mistake. Stick to the 22 ft-lb spec."]},{"id":"bronco6g-oil-change-2-7","title":"Oil Change \u2014 2.7L EcoBoost V6","vehicle":"bronco6g","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"oil","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.75,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":70,"high":110},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","13mm socket","24mm or 27mm cap wrench for cartridge housing","drain pan","torque wrench","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","cartridge oil filter familiarity"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2021-2026"],"tags":["service","fluids","ecoboost","2-7l"],"parts":[{"name":"Motorcraft FL-2062A cartridge oil filter","part_number":"FL-2062A","vendor":"Ford / Amazon","price_estimate_usd":14},{"name":"7 qt Motorcraft 5W-30 full synthetic (WSS-M2C961-A1)","vendor":"Ford / Walmart","price_estimate_usd":50},{"name":"Drain plug crush washer","vendor":"Motorcraft","price_estimate_usd":3}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ Pony Parts \u2014 Motorcraft FL-2062A cartridge filter for 2.7L Bronco","url":"https://www.cjponyparts.com/ford-motorcraft-oil-filter-cartridge-2-7l-bronco/p/FL2062A/"},{"title":"Bronco Nation \u2014 recommended fluids, filters, and components for the 2021 Bronco","url":"https://thebronconation.com/the-recommended-fluids-filters-and-components-of-the-2021-ford-bronco/"},{"title":"Autoevolution \u2014 2021 Ford Bronco engine oil change guide","url":"https://www.autoevolution.com/news/2021-ford-bronco-engine-oil-change-guide-168666.html"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The 2.7L EcoBoost V6 takes 7.0 quarts of 5W-30 full synthetic with a Motorcraft FL-2062A cartridge filter \u2014 a 60-minute service that the cartridge housing makes a touch finickier than the 2.3L's spin-on.","description":"The 2.7L \"Nano\" V6 in the Outer Banks, Badlands, and Wildtrak Broncos is a strong engine \u2014 twin-turbo direct-injection V6 with port injection added, plenty of low-end torque, and proven across the F-150 program since 2015. Treat it like any other modern boosted gasoline V6: full synthetic, 5,000-mile intervals if you do any off-road work or live somewhere hot, and don't run cheap fluid. There is an active 2.7L EcoBoost engine knock lawsuit covering certain 2021\u20132022 builds \u2014 keeping documented oil-change records protects you if a warranty claim becomes necessary.\n\nThe factory fill is Motorcraft Full Synthetic 5W-30 to spec WSS-M2C961-A1. The filter is a cartridge, not a spin-on \u2014 it lives in a plastic-topped housing on the top of the engine, driver side, between the intake and the firewall. The cap takes a 24mm or 27mm cap wrench depending on production date. Replace the housing O-ring every other service (it ships in the FL-2062A box).\n\nPark on level ground and warm the engine 5 minutes. Pop the hood, locate the filter housing, and remove the cap with the cap wrench \u2014 pull the old cartridge straight up. Wipe the housing clean. Down below, raise the front, set stands, pull the splash shield (8mm bolts and push pins), and remove the 13mm drain plug at the rear of the oil pan. Let it drain a full five minutes \u2014 the V6 pan holds more than the 2.3L and the last quart matters. Reinstall the drain plug with a new crush washer at 22 ft-lbs.\n\nDrop the new cartridge into the housing, install a fresh O-ring on the cap, and torque the cap to 18 ft-lbs (it will click into a hard stop \u2014 don't crank it). Refill with 7.0 quarts of 5W-30 synthetic. Start the engine, watch the oil pressure light extinguish in under three seconds, run it 30 seconds, shut down, and check the dipstick after a one-minute settle. Reset the oil life monitor through SYNC.","steps":["Warm engine 5 minutes, then shut off.","Open hood. Locate the cartridge filter housing on top of the engine.","Remove the cap with cap wrench (24 or 27mm); lift out the old cartridge.","Lift the front and set jack stands.","Remove the splash shield (8mm + push pins).","Drain through the 13mm pan plug onto a pan; wait 5 minutes.","Install drain plug with new crush washer at 22 ft-lbs.","Reinstall splash shield. Lower the truck.","Drop new FL-2062A cartridge into housing.","Install new O-ring on cap; reinstall cap and torque to 18 ft-lbs.","Refill with 7.0 quarts of 5W-30 full synthetic.","Start engine; verify oil pressure light goes out under 3 seconds.","Run 30 seconds, shut off, recheck dipstick after 1 minute.","Reset oil life monitor in SYNC."],"pros":["Cartridge filters are environmentally cleaner (you only throw away the paper element) and let you inspect for metal contamination on every service.","7-quart sump and synthetic oil give the 2.7L a generous thermal margin off-road.","Documented oil records strengthen any future warranty claim, including the 2.7L knock action."],"cons":["Cap wrench is an extra tool most new owners don't already have (~$15).","Over-torquing the plastic filter cap will crack it \u2014 stay at 18 ft-lbs.","The cartridge housing is wedged between intake and firewall; large hands struggle without a deep socket or wrench extension."]},{"id":"bronco6g-transfer-case-fluid","title":"Transfer Case Fluid Service","vehicle":"bronco6g","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"drivetrain","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":35,"high":75},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","3/8\" ratchet","10mm hex bit (Allen) socket","torque wrench","fluid pump","drain pan","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2021-2026"],"tags":["service","fluids","drivetrain","transfer case"],"parts":[{"name":"Motorcraft MERCON LV ATF \u2014 2 qts","part_number":"XT-10-QLVC","vendor":"Ford / Amazon","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Drain/fill plug crush washers (if present)","vendor":"Ford","price_estimate_usd":4}],"citations":[{"title":"BlauParts \u2014 2024-2025 Ford Bronco Transfer Case Fluid Change Kit","url":"https://www.blauparts.com/ford-bronco-transfer-case-differential-oil-change-kit-ravenol.html"},{"title":"Bronco6G forum \u2014 Transfer Case Fluid Change","url":"https://www.bronco6g.com/forum/threads/transfer-case-fluid-change.82715/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The 6G transfer case takes 1.2\u20131.6 quarts of MERCON LV ATF (Ford spec WSS-M2C938-A). Capacity depends on which 4x4 system you have \u2014 Part-Time 4x4 is 1.2 L (1.3 qt), Advanced 4x4 with 4A mode is 1.55 L (1.6 qt). Ford lists no scheduled service. Trail-driven trucks should change it at 30,000 miles or after any water crossing that touched the case vent.","description":"The transfer case is one of the most ignored fluids on the truck because Ford never tells you to change it. Trail use changes the math \u2014 heat, water, and shock loading thrash ATF. Plan a first service at 30,000 miles and every 30,000 after, with an extra dump if you fully submerged the case crossing water.\n\nUse the correct fluid. The transfer case wants Motorcraft MERCON LV (WSS-M2C938-A), not gear oil. Substituting heavier oil destroys the clutch pack in the 4A actuator. Stay with the spec.\n\nThe case sits behind the transmission with the fill plug high on the rear face and the drain plug on the bottom. Both are 10mm Allen-head (hex socket) plugs. Loosen the fill plug FIRST \u2014 if it won't break loose, do not drain. Once the fill plug spins free, position your drain pan, remove the drain plug, and let it run. Expect about a quart out, no matter which 4x4 system you have, because ATF clings to the case internals.\n\nReinstall the drain plug torqued to roughly 20 ft-lbs (the service manual lists 27 N\u00b7m). Pump fresh MERCON LV into the fill hole until it weeps back out \u2014 that's the correct level. The case fills from the side, so do not overfill chasing a measured capacity. Reinstall the fill plug at the same torque. Drive 5 miles and recheck for seep.\n\nIf your truck has Advanced 4x4 (the 4A \"auto\" mode), the case has a clutch pack that engages and disengages while driving. Old or contaminated fluid in that pack causes hesitation when 4A swaps drive between front and rear axles. Owners who notice a brief shudder when 4A is selected typically fix it with a fluid swap.","steps":["Park level. Lift the truck so the case sits level. Set jack stands.","Identify the fill plug (high on rear face) and drain plug (bottom). Both are 10mm hex.","Loosen the fill plug FIRST. Do not crack the drain until the fill plug spins.","Position drain pan. Remove the drain plug. Let it drain 10 minutes.","Inspect old fluid \u2014 milky means water intrusion; metal flakes mean clutch wear.","Reinstall drain plug at ~20 ft-lbs.","Pump fresh MERCON LV into the fill hole until it weeps back out the threads.","Reinstall fill plug at ~20 ft-lbs.","Drive 5 miles and recheck both plugs for seep."],"pros":["Cheap insurance \u2014 two quarts of ATF and an hour heads off a $4,000 transfer case rebuild.","Detects water intrusion early. Milky fluid after a crossing tells you the vent or seals need attention before the case grenades.","The job uses basic tools. No specialty equipment, no shop visit."],"cons":["Ford gives no scheduled interval, so it gets skipped by every owner who only does what the dash tells them.","4A \"auto\" mode trucks are pickier about fluid quality than Part-Time-only trucks; old fluid causes engagement shudder.","The fill plug threads bite hard. Use anti-seize sparingly on reinstall so the next service isn't a fight."]},{"id":"bronco6g-hoss-overview","title":"HOSS Suspension Overview \u2014 Sasquatch vs Base","vehicle":"bronco6g","category":"suspension","subcategory":"factory-systems","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":0.5},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":[],"skills_required":["familiarity with Bronco trim levels"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2021-2026"],"tags":["suspension","factory","bilstein","hoss","sasquatch","badlands"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Bronco Nation \u2014 Bronco's Boss HOSS System: 4 Levels of Performance","url":"https://thebronconation.com/broncos-boss-hoss-system-4-levels-of-performance/"},{"title":"Autoblog \u2014 Ford Bronco Suspension Deep Dive","url":"https://www.autoblog.com/features/ford-bronco-suspension-deep-dive"},{"title":"ShockSurplus \u2014 Ultimate Ford Bronco Shocks & Suspension Guide","url":"https://www.shocksurplus.com/blogs/news/ultimate-ford-bronco-shock-buying-guide"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Ford ships the 6G with four distinct HOSS suspension levels. HOSS 1.0 (base) runs twin-tube shocks. HOSS 2.0 (Badlands, Wildtrak, any Sasquatch-package truck) runs Bilstein position-sensitive monotubes with remote reservoirs and internal end-stops. HOSS 3.0 is the Bronco Raptor's Fox Live Valve setup. HOSS 4.0 came with the 2024 Bronco Raptor refresh. Knowing which one is under your truck dictates what aftermarket parts actually fit.","description":"The HOSS naming gets confusing because trim level and shock package don't always match. Sasquatch is a package, not a trim \u2014 you can order Sasquatch on a Base, Big Bend, Outer Banks, Wildtrak, or Badlands. Sasquatch automatically pulls in the Bilstein HOSS 2.0 dampers, the 35-inch Goodyear tires, the high-clearance fender flares, the 4.7 final drive, and the front and rear electronic lockers. Badlands trim includes the same Bilstein dampers but only the rear locker as standard \u2014 front locker requires adding Sasquatch.\n\nHOSS 1.0 (Base, Big Bend, Outer Banks without Sasquatch) runs twin-tube shocks tuned more for on-road compliance than articulation. The truck still drives well off-road but tops out earlier and lacks the bottom-out resistance for high-speed dirt. Aftermarket replacement is easy and cheap because you're swapping common twin-tube geometry.\n\nHOSS 2.0 is the part you actually want for any meaningful off-road use. The Bilstein position-sensitive end-stop control valve (ESCV) dampers stiffen progressively as the shock approaches full compression or full extension \u2014 that's what \"position-sensitive\" means. The middle of the travel range is plush; the last inch on either end ramps up hydraulic resistance to prevent metal-on-metal bottom-out. The remote reservoirs add oil volume so the shock doesn't fade under sustained high-frequency work.\n\nHOSS 3.0 is Bronco Raptor-only \u2014 Fox Live Valve internal bypass shocks with electronically variable damping. Different mounts, different geometry, not a drop-in for non-Raptor trucks.\n\nThere was a recall on early HOSS 2.0 trucks (model years 2021\u20132023, roughly 150,000 units) for rear shock reservoir bracket failures. If you bought a used Badlands or Sasquatch, confirm the recall was performed by entering your VIN at ford.com/support. The fixed brackets are easy to spot \u2014 they're noticeably beefier than the original.\n\nThe aftermarket replacement question depends on what you're trying to do. For HOSS 1.0 trucks, dropping in HOSS 2.0 Bilsteins or aftermarket monotubes (Icon, Old Man Emu, Zone, RPG) is a real upgrade. For HOSS 2.0 trucks, stay with Bilsteins or step up to Fox 2.5 / King 2.5 if you're chasing high-speed desert capability \u2014 there's no point downgrading to a generic monotube.","steps":["Check your build sheet or window sticker for \"HOSS\" or \"Sasquatch.\"","Look at the rear shocks: smooth black body with a remote reservoir line = HOSS 2.0 or 3.0; plain twin-tube body = HOSS 1.0.","Check the dash hero switches. A row of 5\u20136 buttons including front-locker and sway-disconnect indicates Badlands and/or Sasquatch.","Run your VIN at ford.com/support to confirm any shock-related recalls have been performed.","Plan modifications based on what you have \u2014 don't buy 37s on HOSS 1.0 without upgrading the dampers first."],"pros":["Knowing which HOSS package you have prevents wasted aftermarket spending.","HOSS 2.0 Bilsteins are genuinely well-engineered factory dampers \u2014 many trucks need no shock upgrade until 50,000+ miles or until going to 37s.","Position-sensitive damping gives you a softer on-road ride and stiffer end-stops than any single-rate shock could."],"cons":["HOSS 1.0 trucks need a shock upgrade before any aggressive off-road use. The factory twin-tubes fade fast.","The HOSS 2.0 recall affected a lot of trucks \u2014 verify before buying used.","HOSS 3.0 (Raptor) parts are not cross-compatible with HOSS 2.0; they share a name and not much else."]},{"id":"bronco6g-sway-bar-disconnect","title":"Sway Bar Disconnect \u2014 Badlands Feature","vehicle":"bronco6g","category":"suspension","subcategory":"factory-systems","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.1,"high":0.25},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":[],"skills_required":["familiarity with hero switch layout"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2021-2026"],"tags":["suspension","factory","badlands","sway bar","off-road"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Ford Support \u2014 How to disconnect the front stabilizer bar","url":"https://www.ford.com/support/how-tos/more-vehicle-topics/engine-and-transmission/bronco-how-to-disconnect-the-front-stabilizer-bar/"},{"title":"Bronco Nation \u2014 2021 Front Stabilizer Bar Disconnection How-To","url":"https://thebronconation.com/2021-ford-bronco-front-stabilizer-bar-disconnection-how-to/"},{"title":"Bronco6G forum \u2014 Electronic Sway Bar disconnect on a NON-Badlands","url":"https://www.bronco6g.com/forum/threads/electronic-sway-bar-disconnect-on-a-non-badlands.83745/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The Badlands-only electronic front sway-bar disconnect is a single hero switch on the dash. Press it once while in 4H or 4L below 20 mph. The amber bar icon means disconnected. Above 20 mph the bar auto-reconnects (gray icon = standby, ready to disconnect again when you slow down). It is the single biggest off-road advantage in the Badlands package and it costs nothing to use.","description":"The disconnect lives on the bank of \"hero switches\" above the center screen. On a Badlands or any Sasquatch-equipped truck, it's the button with the icon showing two wheels connected by a curved bar. Press once.\n\nThe system has three states. **Connected** (no icon): you're driving normally; the sway bar is bolted in like a normal vehicle. **Standby** (gray bar icon): you've requested disconnect and the system is waiting for the conditions to be met. **Disconnected** (amber bar icon): the hydraulic actuator inside the sway bar has unclamped and the two halves of the bar can rotate independently. That last state is what lets the front wheels articulate freely \u2014 one wheel can drop into a rut while the other climbs a rock without lifting the truck's body the way a connected bar would.\n\nThree conditions must be met for disconnect to engage. The transfer case must be in 4H or 4L. The vehicle speed must be below 20 mph. The steering wheel must be relatively centered (sharp steering inputs delay the disconnect signal). If you press the button at 35 mph on the highway, nothing happens \u2014 the icon will glow gray indicating \"standby\" and the bar will disconnect the moment you slow below 20.\n\nAuto-reconnect is a safety feature, not a bug. Above 20 mph the sway bar slams back together because driving with a disconnected front bar at speed makes the truck dangerously rolly. The gray standby icon stays lit when you exceed 20, so the moment you slow back down for the next obstacle the bar disconnects again automatically without re-pressing the button. To exit disconnect mode entirely, press the button a second time.\n\nNon-Badlands and non-Sasquatch 6Gs have a mechanical, non-electronic front sway bar. You can manually disconnect those with aftermarket quick-disconnect end links from RPG, Zone, or Steer Smarts, but it's a get-out-of-the-truck-and-unbolt-it operation, not a button push.\n\nA common point of confusion: the disconnect only affects the FRONT sway bar. The 6G has no factory rear-bar disconnect \u2014 the rear bar stays connected. For most trail use that's fine because the front axle does most of the articulation work, but rock crawlers eventually swap the rear bar for an adjustable or removable aftermarket bar to get the rest of the available droop.\n\nThe hydraulic actuator inside the bar is sealed and not user-serviceable. Trail damage to the actuator usually means a full bar replacement. The system does throw codes \u2014 if the icon stays gray and refuses to go amber even at low speed in 4H, that's a sensor or actuator failure, not user error.","steps":["Identify the sway-disconnect button on the dash hero switches (curved bar icon between two wheels).","Shift to 4H or 4L. Slow below 20 mph.","Center the steering wheel.","Press the button once. Watch the dash icon turn amber.","Use the disconnect through the obstacle.","When done, press the button again to exit disconnect mode. The bar will re-engage automatically.","If the icon stays gray, check that you are in 4H/4L and below 20 mph. Persistent failure means a sensor or actuator issue \u2014 pull DTCs."],"pros":["Front-axle articulation roughly doubles with the bar disconnected. The difference on a rocky climb is immediately obvious.","Press-button operation means you can disconnect mid-obstacle without stopping and crawling under the truck.","Auto-reconnect above 20 mph prevents the most common mistake \u2014 forgetting to reconnect before highway driving."],"cons":["Badlands and Sasquatch trucks only. Other 6Gs need an aftermarket quick-disconnect or a manual-unbolt workaround.","Sealed actuator means trail damage to the bar is expensive. Treat it like any factory electronic component.","Rear bar stays connected. Rock crawlers will eventually want an adjustable aftermarket rear bar to free up the rest of the articulation."]}];